WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.766 --> 00:00:02.401 line:0%
-It derails my work.

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:04.103 line:0%
-I know. I'm sorry.

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:06.639 line:0%
-I just --
I'm trying to look out

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:10.076 line:0%
why you would choose
to hurt me so deliberately.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:14.347 line:0%
-No, I -- I mean, I just wanted
to hang out with my friends.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.548 line:0%
-Right.
-I wasn't --

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:18.818 line:0%
-So you want to be here
with them at my expense?

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.853 line:0%
You're incredibly selfish.

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:22.488
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:24.290
We're here with Anna Kendrick,
everybody.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.460
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.095
-And now for
a complete tonal shift.

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.630
-It was a real tonal shift,
yeah.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.265
-It brings the room down even.

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.466
-And, now, that's a comedy?

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:37.437
-Oh, it's about psychological
abuse. It's hilarious.

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:38.938
You guys.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.641
-Now, this -- I do want
to ask one more question

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:44.009
about directing
before I ask about this film

00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:47.379
where you were an actor in
and you directed yourself.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:49.716
You are an actor....
but what did you --

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.618
Did you learn anything --
I think, over the years,

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:53.352
directors would love
to know the kind of notes

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:55.087
actors like to receive.

00:00:55.087 --> 00:00:57.590
And you having been
on the other side first,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.959
how did you find
giving those to actors was?

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:02.295
-I mean, I thought -- Well, you
know, I hoped I was okay at it,

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:07.700
but I did find that I would
just give really direct notes.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.569
And I would give somebody
a note,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:12.205
and then like three people
from the set, from the crew,

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.874
would be like, "I didn't know
you could say that to an actor."

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:18.277
I'm like,
"Okay, we are like children.

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:20.546
We're barely people. I get it."

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:24.116
But, like, there's this feeling
that we're going to, like --

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:27.219
like explode into dust
like "Avengers Endgame"

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.221
if you say, "Can you just
do it a little faster?"

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:31.958
That's all. Like, that's all
I said to people, was like,

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:34.427
"Hey, maybe just
a little quieter or something."

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.462
-Well, that's a helpful note
for an actor, right?

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:38.597
-I agree. I agree.
-They know exactly how to do it.

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:40.966
-Like, I feel like sometimes
if somebody just wants me

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:43.369
to do something specific,
they tell me this long story

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.905
about my character's grandmother
or something,

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.407
and I'm trying to decipher,
like,

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:49.441
"Did you just mean quieter?

00:01:49.441 --> 00:01:52.945
You can just say it, babe.
I'll be good."

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.582
-So, in this film,
you play a Canadian.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.550
You shot this at a remote cabin.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.819
And I'm wondering,
when you play a Canadian,

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.188
do you try to affect
a Canadian accent?

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:07.593
-I think nobody told me to.
Nobody wanted that from me.

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:10.095
But I did --
There were a couple times

00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:12.599
where I would, like, try
to sneak in a, like,

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:14.767
"Oh, you're having trouble
with that, Eh?"

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.169
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:19.271
And, like, the entire cast
and crew was Canadian,

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:22.742
so they're like, "Baby, no.
We don't need to do that.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.677
We don't need to
do that today, no."

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:28.447
-What was it about this
psychological sort of thriller

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:30.216
that drew you to it
in the first place?

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:33.786
-Yeah, I mean,
it's a really challenging movie,

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.088
and it's about emotional abuse,

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:38.090
which is a hard thing
to even put into words,

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.959
let alone put in a movie,

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.295
and I think the movie's not
really what you expect it to be.

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.063
It's almost got this, like,
mystery structure

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.998
where it unfolds really slowly.

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:48.634
And people have been
really kind about the film,

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:52.238
and I think a compliment
that I've been getting recently

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.173
is that people will talk about
it and they'll be like,

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.708
"And I loved, like, you know,

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.610
almost how little dialogue
there was."

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.779
And you can only get
that compliment so many times

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.513
before you're like,
"Is it my shrill voice?

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:03.316
Is that the problem?"

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:06.252
You're like, "I loved the part
where you shut up.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:08.955
It was, mwah, your finest work."

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:10.957
-I'm sure that's not
what they were saying.

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:13.326
This is very exciting.
You went to a wedding in Italy,

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.361
but you did not go to Italy
to go to a wedding.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.863
-Okay, so, yeah.

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.064
I went to Italy this summer.

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:20.365
It was my first time.
Italy's great.

00:03:20.365 --> 00:03:21.534
-Italy's great.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.103
-Have you guys heard
about Italy?

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.572
I hadn't, because
I am not well-traveled.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:28.173
But it was amazing.

00:03:28.173 --> 00:03:32.711
And I ended up in Florence,
and this --

00:03:32.711 --> 00:03:34.814
The actor Jay Ellis,
who's from --

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.883
we love from "Insecure"
and "Top Gun." Lovely guy.

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:40.486
I had met him like twice.

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:43.222
And the place I was staying
in Florence --

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:45.758
across the street,
he was having the, like,

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:48.027
night-before reception
for his wedding.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.363
And he found out
I was across the street.

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:52.197
He was like, "Well, you have to
come to the reception.

00:03:52.197 --> 00:03:54.367
It's just so crazy
that you're here right now."

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.469
And so I was like,
"Yeah, of course, of course."

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.303
And I went, and then all night,
he's saying,

00:03:58.303 --> 00:03:59.838
"You should really come
to the wedding tomorrow,"

00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:01.940
which I was like, "No.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:03.943
So sweet of you to offer.

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:06.412
I understand you feel obligated
to be polite and offer,

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:07.647
but of course not."

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.815
And he kept insisting,
and I was like,

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:13.185
"No, no, I don't want to be,
like, a part of a fight

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:15.955
that you and your wife get into,
in five years where she's like,

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:17.356
'And another thing!

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:20.693
You invite what's-her-face
the night before the wedding."

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.162
No. No.

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:27.133
And as I'm heading out,
he asks me again.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:28.434
I said,
"No, that's so sweet of you.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.336
No, thank you.
I couldn't possibly."

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:33.039
And I said, "Well, you know,

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:35.074
I heard the dress code
is earth tone,

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.608
and I didn't even pack
anything earth tone."

00:04:36.608 --> 00:04:39.111
And he said, "Well, Anna,
you're rich as [bleep],

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:40.980
so go buy something."

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.084
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:50.289
Which -- So now
I'm the cheap ass [bleep]...

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.958
-Yeah.
-...if I don't go buy something

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:54.793
to go to the wedding.

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:57.530
So the next morning,
I, like, went to Zara --

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.065
'cause I'm very rich --
and I just bought,

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:03.302
like, the first thing I found
and I went to the wedding.

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:04.837
And I did cry like a baby,

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:06.906
even though I was
totally crashing the wedding.

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:08.374
Like, you would have thought

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.409
that this is my oldest friend
in the world, I cried so much.

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:12.144
-I think it's very --
it's allowed that you cried.

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:13.980
You're in Florence, Italy.
It's a very romantic place.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.481
Obviously, you'd just
blown a lot of money

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.552
on an outfit you were
never going to wear again.

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:22.054
Congratulations on the movies.

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:23.389
-Thank you.
-Both of them.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.091
And it's so nice
to have you here in person.

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:26.926
-It's always so great to be
here. Thank you for having me.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:29.094
-We love having you.
Anna Kendrick, everybody.

00:05:29.094 --> 00:05:31.497
"Alice, Darling" opens
exclusively at AMC Theaters

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.765
nationwide on January 20th.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:34.567
we'll be right back
with John Larroquette.

